<!--
problem.md — THE ONLY TASK CONTENT THE MODEL SEES, besides the files in
simulation/ (which the container appends to the prompt automatically).

On Taiga this is either pasted into the Task Prompt form field (preferred) or
left here as problem.md and shipped. The form wins when both exist.

WRITE THIS LAST — Step 8 in INSTRUCTIONS.md. Not before the answer is verified
and the wrong-path solver has been shown to fail.

YOU write this, in your own words. Per the AI Use Policy, Claude must never
draft the solver-facing prompt.

A forward task hides NOTHING about the system — disclose it fully, including
every file in simulation/. What you must not disclose is WHICH SETTINGS ARE
ADEQUATE. That decision is the task.

CHECKLIST before saving:
  [ ] The answer value appears nowhere in this file
  [ ] The system is fully specified — geometry, parameters, conditions, regime
  [ ] Every file in simulation/ is named and its role explained
  [ ] No hint that the default mesh/resolution/solver is inadequate
  [ ] No "you should refine", "check convergence", "use the transient solver"
  [ ] No method name that hands over the solver choice
  [ ] Mesh / method / stopping decisions left entirely to the model
  [ ] Not a canonical setup whose answer can be recalled from literature
  [ ] Clear of the validator's leakage vocabulary — INSTRUCTIONS.md lists the
      words it flags in solver-visible text
  [ ] Answer shape matches golden/expected.json
  [ ] Tolerance and units stated
  [ ] Any convention that matters (sign, reference quantity, denominator) IS stated
  [ ] Explicit instruction to call submit_answer(answer)

State the CONVENTION, so a correct solver isn't guessing what you want, but never
the METHOD, so the solver still has to decide how to get there. Both matter, and
they pull in opposite directions.

DELETE THIS COMMENT BLOCK before you upload — problem.md ships as written.
-->

# <Title>

<!-- Describe the system completely: geometry, material or rate parameters,
     boundary and initial conditions, the physical regime, and the quantity to
     report. Nothing here is secret — withholding part of the setup turns this
     into a guessing game rather than a method decision.

     State the regime factually ("the flow is at Reynolds number 100") without
     drawing the conclusion that follows from it ("...so the wake is unsteady
     and you will need a transient solver"). The first is the setup. The second
     is the answer to the question being asked. -->

# Input files

<!-- Name every file in simulation/ and what it is (case folder, mesh, netlist,
     config). Say nothing about which of its settings are adequate. -->

# Your task

<!-- State exactly which quantity to report, and that you expect the model to
     run the domain tool itself. One paragraph. Say nothing about how to
     configure the run. -->

# Output format

<!-- The submission shape, matching golden/expected.json element for element and
     in the same order. State the tolerance, the units, and any convention that
     affects the number. -->

Submit your answer via `submit_answer(answer)`.
